not seen; Khaleb Yordan and Rose Ann Rowlett also involved in the field; apparently two birds, nearer (after 1:45) and farther; seemingly on or near the forest floor (primary forest at the very base of the Anaso Track); beyond failing to see it, we are nervous about the ID on a rare bird for which there is little comparative material; Roger McNeil recordings on Macaulay start with a 'stutter' (excited bird?) not present here; BirdNET offers 0.009 on Asian Emerald Dove (unlikely at this spot) and 0.001 on Superb Fruit-Dove (which we heard at the same spot), i.e., it is stumped; interval shortened at 2:42 from 0610 hours, both birds singing through much of the middle of this section; much dripping from overnight rain; normalized and filtered on both sides of a vehicle in the middle; a relatively clear, representative section of one bird is after 2:00; 
Still nervous about the ID, I asked James Eaton for an opinion, and he further involved Khaleb, Rob, Mike, and Boas, colleagues at Birdtour Asia (thank you all, for the input), and the consensus was "fairly confident" in the ID, along with no one having an alternative species to suggest.  Still, until more is known about this bird, some lingering question mark should remain.  
Subspecies based on a range description of "north-central".
